# **DataPrepper**

`DataPrepper` parses instant-messenger chat logs into normalized JSON. It runs
several format-specific parsers over each input file, picks the one that extracts
the most records, and writes one normalized output file per input containing
`{author, timestamp, message}` records (JSON Lines / NDJSON).

Supported log formats: **AIM**, **Facebook Messenger**, **MSN**, **Google Voice**,
and **Google Chat** (HTML, plus JSON for Google Chat).

## Dependencies

- Python 3
- [`beautifulsoup4`](https://pypi.org/project/beautifulsoup4/) (`bs4`)
- [`python-dateutil`](https://pypi.org/project/python-dateutil/) (`dateutil`)

```
pip install beautifulsoup4 python-dateutil
```

## Directory layout

```
userdata/
├── compressed/chat/                      # input archives (.tar.gz/.tgz/.tar.xz/.zip)
├── raw/chat/{messenger}/{owner}/{participant}/<file>   # extracted chat logs
├── transformed/chat/                     # parsed output (one file per input)
└── reports/                              # JSON run reports
```

## Usage

### `./01_extract`

Decompress every archive under `userdata/compressed/` into `userdata/raw/`,
recursing into nested archives. This is the ingest step that populates
`userdata/raw/chat/`.

### `./02_transform`

The primary parser. Walks `userdata/raw/chat/{messenger}/{owner}/{participant}/`,
runs all parsers on each `.html`/`.htm` file, selects the best result, corrects
record dates from the filename, de-duplicates identical conversations by content
hash, and writes `.ndjson` output to `userdata/transformed/chat/` named
`{messenger}-{owner}-{participant}-{stem}.ndjson`.

A detailed JSON run report is written to `userdata/reports/parse_report_*.json`,
including files that failed to parse (grouped by reason) and **low-yield** files
whose output is a suspiciously small fraction of the source size (possible
incomplete parse).

### `./newparse.py`

A lighter-weight variant of the same idea (6 parsers, no date-correction or
dedup). Writes `.json` output to `userdata/transformed/chat/` and a report to
`userdata/reports/newparse_report_*.json`. Its stderr summary reports the number
of files **seen**, **attempted**, and that **produced output**, plus the lists of
unparsed and low-yield files.

## `xform/`

Pluggable per-format parsers. `base_parser.py` defines the parser interface; each
`*_parser.py` implements extraction for one log format. Adding support for a new
format means dropping in another parser module and registering it in the entry
script.
